{"content_hash": "7ff73e60bb1deece", "version_id": 153, "article_id": 16, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "AI Developer Builds Free Copies of Adobe Apps", "title_zh": "AI开发者用开源软件挑战Adobe", "topic": "tech", "source": "arstechnica", "source_url": "https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/software-is-over-bold-ai-developer-takes-aim-at-adobe-with-open-source-clones/", "image": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(343, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(21, 46%, 48%)", "label": "TECH", "seed": "2a1fc9572154"}, "published_date": "2026-10-07", "word_count": 271, "reading_minutes": 1.4, "lead": "A software developer named Brandon Thomas has used AI to create free versions of Adobe's popular apps. He says they will soon work just as well as the paid originals.", "lead_zh": "一位名叫布兰登·托马斯的软件开发者利用AI做出了Adobe热门软件的自由版本。他说这些软件很快就能和付费原版一样好用。", "preview": "For years, many people have wanted to avoid Adobe's expensive Creative Suite. Although some free alternatives already exist, one developer i", "preview_zh": "多年来，许多人想避开Adobe昂贵的Creative Suite。虽然已经有一些免费的替代品，但现在有一位开发者用AI更逼真地复制Adobe的软件。Artcraft去年作为一个编辑AI生成图像的工具起步。然而，上周末，它展示了七款新的开源应用，外观和功能都像Adobe的Photo", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 143, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e099b16/content/audio/16/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 110.788, "size_bytes": 668592, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 319, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@e099b16/content/data/versions/153.json", "paragraphs": ["For years, many people have wanted to avoid Adobe's expensive Creative Suite. Although some free alternatives already exist, one developer is now using AI to copy Adobe's software more closely. Artcraft started last year as a tool for editing AI-made images. However, over the weekend, it showed seven new open source apps that look and work like Adobe's Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat Pro.", "The developer, Brandon Thomas, said he used Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5 to build these apps in the Rust programming language. He promised on Reddit that they would reach full feature parity within a month. Later, he changed his words on Hacker News, saying that 99% parity would take months, not years. Many commenters pointed out that the apps are still in a very early alpha state and have many problems.", "Thomas explained that he was inspired after being charged a cancellation fee one too many times. The apps are free under MIT and Apache licenses, and they will be funded by selling access to the Artcraft visual AI model. This way, the project will not become an orphaned small-team effort. He also said he has a team that builds popular software for filmmakers, so the work is fully funded.", "Using AI to reverse engineer software is not new, and it is usually legal. However, the Artcraft apps could face legal trouble if they copy Adobe's look too closely. Thomas believes that soon everyday developers can recreate open source versions of paid software. \"We'll replace Google Search, we'll build our own smartphones,\" he wrote. \"Software is over,\" he added, claiming he had \"one-shotted Photoshop.\""], "paragraphs_zh": ["多年来，许多人想避开Adobe昂贵的Creative Suite。虽然已经有一些免费的替代品，但现在有一位开发者用AI更逼真地复制Adobe的软件。Artcraft去年作为一个编辑AI生成图像的工具起步。然而，上周末，它展示了七款新的开源应用，外观和功能都像Adobe的Photoshop、Illustrator、Premiere、Lightroom、After Effects、InDesign和Acrobat Pro。", "开发者布兰登·托马斯说，他使用Anthropic的Claude Opus 5.5，用Rust编程语言构建了这些应用。他在Reddit上承诺，它们将在一个月内达到完整的功能对等。后来，他在Hacker News上改口说，99%的对等需要几个月，而不是几年。许多评论者指出，这些应用仍处于非常早期的alpha阶段，存在许多问题。", "托马斯解释说，他的灵感来自被收取太多次取消费。这些应用在MIT和Apache许可下免费，并将通过销售Artcraft视觉AI模型的访问权限来获得资金。这样，项目就不会成为一个被遗弃的小团队项目。他还说，他有一个为电影制作人构建流行软件的团队，所以这项工作资金充足。", "使用AI逆向工程软件并不新鲜，而且通常是合法的。然而，如果Artcraft应用过于接近地复制Adobe的外观，它们可能会面临法律麻烦。托马斯相信，很快普通开发者就能重新创建付费软件的开源版本。“我们将取代谷歌搜索，我们将建造自己的智能手机，”他写道。“软件已死，”他补充说，并声称他“一击即中Photoshop”。"], "body": "For years, many people have wanted to avoid Adobe's expensive Creative Suite. Although some free alternatives already exist, one developer is now using AI to copy Adobe's software more closely. Artcraft started last year as a tool for editing AI-made images. However, over the weekend, it showed seven new open source apps that look and work like Adobe's Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat Pro.\n\nThe developer, Brandon Thomas, said he used Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5 to build these apps in the Rust programming language. He promised on Reddit that they would reach full feature parity within a month. Later, he changed his words on Hacker News, saying that 99% parity would take months, not years. Many commenters pointed out that the apps are still in a very early alpha state and have many problems.\n\nThomas explained that he was inspired after being charged a cancellation fee one too many times. The apps are free under MIT and Apache licenses, and they will be funded by selling access to the Artcraft visual AI model. This way, the project will not become an orphaned small-team effort. He also said he has a team that builds popular software for filmmakers, so the work is fully funded.\n\nUsing AI to reverse engineer software is not new, and it is usually legal. However, the Artcraft apps could face legal trouble if they copy Adobe's look too closely. Thomas believes that soon everyday developers can recreate open source versions of paid software. \"We'll replace Google Search, we'll build our own smartphones,\" he wrote. \"Software is over,\" he added, claiming he had \"one-shotted Photoshop.\"", "body_zh": "多年来，许多人想避开Adobe昂贵的Creative Suite。虽然已经有一些免费的替代品，但现在有一位开发者用AI更逼真地复制Adobe的软件。Artcraft去年作为一个编辑AI生成图像的工具起步。然而，上周末，它展示了七款新的开源应用，外观和功能都像Adobe的Photoshop、Illustrator、Premiere、Lightroom、After Effects、InDesign和Acrobat Pro。\n\n开发者布兰登·托马斯说，他使用Anthropic的Claude Opus 5.5，用Rust编程语言构建了这些应用。他在Reddit上承诺，它们将在一个月内达到完整的功能对等。后来，他在Hacker News上改口说，99%的对等需要几个月，而不是几年。许多评论者指出，这些应用仍处于非常早期的alpha阶段，存在许多问题。\n\n托马斯解释说，他的灵感来自被收取太多次取消费。这些应用在MIT和Apache许可下免费，并将通过销售Artcraft视觉AI模型的访问权限来获得资金。这样，项目就不会成为一个被遗弃的小团队项目。他还说，他有一个为电影制作人构建流行软件的团队，所以这项工作资金充足。\n\n使用AI逆向工程软件并不新鲜，而且通常是合法的。然而，如果Artcraft应用过于接近地复制Adobe的外观，它们可能会面临法律麻烦。托马斯相信，很快普通开发者就能重新创建付费软件的开源版本。“我们将取代谷歌搜索，我们将建造自己的智能手机，”他写道。“软件已死，”他补充说，并声称他“一击即中Photoshop”。", "text": "For years, many people have wanted to avoid Adobe's expensive Creative Suite. Although some free alternatives already exist, one developer is now using AI to copy Adobe's software more closely. Artcraft started last year as a tool for editing AI-made images. However, over the weekend, it showed seven new open source apps that look and work like Adobe's Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat Pro. The developer, Brandon Thomas, said he used Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5 to build these apps in the Rust programming language. He promised on Reddit that they would reach full feature parity within a month. Later, he changed his words on Hacker News, saying that 99% parity would take months, not years. Many commenters pointed out that the apps are still in a very early alpha state and have many problems. Thomas explained that he was inspired after being charged a cancellation fee one too many times. The apps are free under MIT and Apache licenses, and they will be funded by selling access to the Artcraft visual AI model. This way, the project will not become an orphaned small-team effort. He also said he has a team that builds popular software for filmmakers, so the work is fully funded. Using AI to reverse engineer software is not new, and it is usually legal. However, the Artcraft apps could face legal trouble if they copy Adobe's look too closely. Thomas believes that soon everyday developers can recreate open source versions of paid software. \"We'll replace Google Search, we'll build our own smartphones,\" he wrote. \"Software is over,\" he added, claiming he had \"one-shotted Photoshop.\"", "vocab": [{"word": "alternative", "pos": "noun", "zh": "替代品", "note": "常与to连用，表示某物的替代选择"}, {"word": "open source", "pos": "adj", "zh": "开源的", "note": "指源代码公开、可自由使用的软件"}, {"word": "feature parity", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "功能对等", "note": "指软件功能与原版完全一致"}, {"word": "alpha", "pos": "noun", "zh": "内部测试版", "note": "软件开发的最早阶段，通常不稳定"}, {"word": "reverse engineer", "pos": "verb", "zh": "逆向工程", "note": "通过分析成品来复制其功能"}, {"word": "cancellation fee", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "取消费用", "note": "取消订阅或服务时收取的费用"}, {"word": "license", "pos": "noun", "zh": "许可证", "note": "法律上允许使用软件的凭证"}, {"word": "fund", "pos": "verb", "zh": "资助", "note": "提供资金支持"}, {"word": "orphaned", "pos": "adj", "zh": "被遗弃的", "note": "指项目无人维护、被放弃"}, {"word": "trade dress", "pos": "noun", "zh": "商业外观", "note": "产品的外观设计，受法律保护"}], "title_original": "“Software is over”: Bold AI developer takes aim at Adobe with open source clones", "summary_original": "Opus-built Creative Cloud alternatives are ambitious, free, and nowhere near finished.", "body_original": "If it quacks like adobe\n“Software is over”: Bold AI developer takes aim at Adobe with open source clones\nOpus-built Creative Cloud alternatives are ambitious, free, and nowhere near finished.\nKyle Orland\n–\nOct 7, 2026 5:56 pm\n|\n74\nThink that's a screen from Photoshop? Look again...\nCredit:\nArtcraft\nThink that's a screen from Photoshop? Look again...\nCredit:\nArtcraft\nText\nsettings\nStory text\nSize\nSmall\nStandard\nLarge\nWidth\n*\nStandard\nWide\nLinks\nStandard\nOrange\n* Subscribers only\nLearn more\nMinimize to nav\nFor a while, those hoping to\navoid the difficulty and expense of dealing with Adobe’s Creative Suite\nhave been able to use\nseveral free and/or open-source alternatives\nthat replicate some or all of those capabilities. Now, one developer is using AI-powered reverse engineering to mimic the look, feel, and functionality of Adobe’s well-known software with what he hopes will soon be perfect fidelity.\nArtcraft\ngot its start\nlast year\nas a “controllable AI for artists” that allows for precise post-generation editing of AI-crafted images and videos. Over the weekend, though, the brand\ndebuted its pivot\nto\na suite of seven open source apps\nrecreating the user interface and tools found in Adobe’s Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat Pro.\nIn\nannouncing those new apps on Reddit\nearlier this week,\nsoftware developer Brandon Thomas\nsaid he used Anthropic’s Claude Opus 5.5 to create “clean-room replacements” for Adobe’s closed source software in Rust (with WebAssembly versions available for use in a browser). And while commenters\non Hacker News\nand\nelsewhere\nhave\ntaken pains\nto point out the new software’s many current shortcomings, Thomas make the grandiose promise that\nthe current “super early alpha” state\nwill soon lead to something just as functional as anything Adobe has ever put out.\n“We’re going to reach 100% [feature] parity within a month,” Thomas\nstated on Reddit\nof his plans to quickly squash bugs and add features with the help of community reports. Days later,\non Hacker News\n, he amended that position slightly to say that “99% parity will take a while, but I’m sure it’ll be measured in months and not years.”\nIs software over?\nThomas, who says he has about a decade of experience working on large-scale Rust projects,\nwrote on Hacker News\nthat he was inspired to create his own open source Adobe software alternatives after being “bit by the dark pattern ‘cancellation fee’ one too many times.” Development of the totally free options offered here under MIT/Apache licenses will be funded, Thomas\nwrote\n, by selling token-based access to\nthe pre-existing Artcraft visual AI model and IDE\n, which is offered as an imagery-generation option inside the apps alongside third-party models. This revenue stream will help ensure these apps aren’t “going to be some orphaned small-team project,” Thomas wrote. “We have a team building popular software for filmmakers, so supporting this is fully funded.”\nThomas is the\nlatest in a long line\nof coders using AI tools to speed up the\nlong-standing but labor-intensive practice\nof reverse engineering (i.e., replicating the functionality of a piece of software without directly copying the code itself). While that practice has\ngenerally been found legally acceptable\n, the ArtCraft apps\ncould still face legal trouble\nif their “trade dress” too closely mimics Adobe’s familiar appearance and interface.\nAfter coding exclusively with AI tools since February, Thomas\nsaid\nhe foresees a near future when everyday developers can re-create open source versions of popular paid software to “take back the internet and own tech forever.”\n“Now you can just create 1:1 functional equivalents, clean room, that are highly performant and cross-platform. Nothing is stopping you,” Thomas wrote. “We’ll replace Google Search, we’ll build our own smartphones, we’ll build better infra. We’ll remove all the ads and de-enshittify the entire tech world.”\n“Software is over,” he gloated in\nan earlier comment\n, claiming to have “one-shotted Photoshop. The whole thing.”\nKyle Orland\nSenior Gaming Editor\nKyle Orland\nSenior Gaming Editor\nKyle Orland has been the Senior Gaming Editor at Ars Technica since 2012, writing primarily about the business, tech, and culture behind video games. He has journalism and computer science degrees from University of Maryland. He once\nwrote a whole book about\nMinesweeper\n.\n74 Comments", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T01:08:45.446425", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "For", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.325, "sm": 100, "em": 325, "cs": 0, "ce": 3, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "years", "s": 0.338, "e": 0.775, "sm": 337, "em": 775, "cs": 4, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": ",", "s": 0.775, "e": 1.05, "sm": 775, "em": 1050, "cs": 9, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "many", "s": 1.05, "e": 1.35, "sm": 1050, "em": 1350, "cs": 11, "ce": 15, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "people", "s": 1.363, "e": 1.712, "sm"